-- title:
--   Node units vanish only for constant data on F_N
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a perfect field and let $N \ge 1$ be a natural number. Write $F_N :=$ `modularFunctionFieldC K N` for the intermediate field of the Laurent series field $K(\!(q)\!)$ over $K$ generated by the two series `jqModC K` (the $q$-expansion of $j$, namely $q^{-1}$ times the integral power series `jNum` with coefficients mapped into $K$) and `jqNModC K N` (its image under the substitution $q \mapsto q^N$). A place of $F_N$ over $K$ is a valuation subring of $F_N$ containing the image of $K$, different from all of $F_N$, and a principal ideal ring. Let $S$ be a finite set of ordered pairs of such places, and let $w : S \to \mathrm{Additive}\,K^\times$ assign a unit of $K$ to each pair. Then the class `GluedPic0.nodeUnit S w`, that is the class of the admissible gluing datum $(0,0,w)$ with both divisor components zero, vanishes in the glued degree-zero Picard group $\mathrm{GluedPic0}(K,F_N,S)$ — the quotient of the group of admissible gluing data (pairs of degree-zero divisors vanishing at the first, respectively second, coordinates of the pairs in $S$, together with a unit datum) by the subgroup of glued principal data — if and only if there is a single $c \in K^\times$ with $w$ constantly equal to $c$.
--
--   This identifies the kernel of the node-unit map for the level-$N$ modular function field over a perfect base field: gluing data supported only at the prescribed nodes are trivial exactly when the gluing units are a common constant, which is the statement that the nodes contribute a torus of the expected rank to the Picard group of a glued curve. It is used in the Čerednik–Drinfeld part of the development, in the construction of two-level semistable specialisations and of correspondences carrying the arithmetic Frobenius on supersingular places.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_nodeUnit_eq_zero_iff_modularFunctionFieldC_of_perfectField.lean

